\title{DEEP and the Out-of-Domain Trick}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Sampling outside the evaluation domain, which appears in deployed provers for reasons that are not the reason usually given. The SoK's claim that the deployed trick and the low-degree-test modification are routinely conflated is exactly the kind of thing I want nailed down here.
}

\p{
    To work through:
    \ul{
        \li{DEEP quotienting: what the quotient is, and what an out-of-domain evaluation buys the analysis.}
        \li{The conjecture on classical list-decodability that DEEP-FRI introduced.}
        \li{The two distinct uses — constraint batching in a STARK versus the low-degree test itself — and which one production code implements.}
        \li{Where the extension field is forced (the out-of-domain point lives there), and the cost.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367).
}
